1. Definitions
================================================================================

"OpenHours" means the unincorporated joint project owned by the Maintainers
named above, together with any successor entity to which the Maintainers
unanimously assign the Software in accordance with this license.

"Maintainer" means each of Emilio Scott, Colin Mcdonald, and Elias
Santillan, or, after a Permitted Transfer under Section 3.3, the person or
entity holding that Maintainer's ownership share subject to this license.

"You" or "Your" means the individual or entity exercising any rights or
performing any acts under this license. If You are acting on behalf of an
organization, "You" includes that organization.

"Service" means any deployed instance, hosted version, integration, fork,
mirror, or production build of the Software, including the production
deployment at openhours.me and any successor domain.

"Major Decision" has the meaning given in Section 2.3.

"Permitted Transfer" has the meaning given in Section 3.3.

================================================================================
2. Joint Ownership and Governance
================================================================================

2.1 Equal, Undivided Joint Ownership.
The Software, and all copyrights, trade secrets, trademark rights in the
OpenHours name and logo, patent rights (if any), database rights, moral
rights to the extent waivable, and all other intellectual property rights
in or to the Software, are owned jointly and in equal undivided shares of
one-third (1/3) each by the three Maintainers. No Maintainer holds a
controlling interest. The Maintainers' relationship under this license is
that of joint owners of intellectual property; nothing in this license
creates a partnership, employer-employee relationship, agency, fiduciary
duty (beyond those imposed by law on co-owners of intellectual property),
or joint venture except as expressly stated.

2.2 Routine Operations.
Each Maintainer, acting alone, may perform routine technical and
operational work that is not a Major Decision, including writing and
merging code, creating branches, deploying to development or staging
environments, fixing bugs, rotating credentials, updating dependencies,
responding to security advisories, triaging incidents, and answering
ordinary user support requests. Each Maintainer is expected to communicate
material routine actions to the others in good faith and to keep
operational records reasonably accessible to the other Maintainers.

2.3 Major Decisions Require Unanimous Consent.
The Maintainers must reach unanimous, documented agreement before taking,
authorizing, or representing to a third party any of the following actions,
each a "Major Decision":

  (a) Granting any license, sublicense, distribution right, or commercial-
      use right in the Software or any material portion of it to any third
      party.
  (b) Selling, assigning, transferring, encumbering, pledging, or granting
      a security interest in all or any part of the Software, the OpenHours
      name, or any associated intellectual property.
  (c) Changing this license, the OpenHours Terms of Service, the OpenHours
      Privacy Policy, or any other binding policy that governs the Service
      or its users.
  (d) Releasing the Software, in whole or in part, under an open-source,
      source-available, or other public license.
  (e) Forming, joining, merging, dissolving, or restructuring any company,
      LLC, partnership, nonprofit, or other legal entity that holds or
      operates the Software.
  (f) Accepting investment, debt, grant funding, or other financing on
      behalf of OpenHours that creates an obligation against the Software
      or against the Maintainers collectively.
  (g) Distributing revenue, equity, profits, or other proceeds derived from
      the Software, or setting compensation for any Maintainer.
  (h) Removing a Maintainer for cause under Section 3.4.
  (i) Adding a new Maintainer or otherwise diluting any Maintainer's
      one-third ownership share.
  (j) Discontinuing, sunsetting, or shutting down the production Service,
      or transferring it to a different operator.
  (k) Initiating or settling any litigation, arbitration, or formal legal
      proceeding in the name of OpenHours or that materially affects
      OpenHours.
  (l) Entering into any contract on behalf of OpenHours that imposes
      obligations longer than ninety (90) days, that limits the
      Maintainers' rights under this license, or that creates financial
      exposure exceeding one thousand U.S. dollars ($1,000).
  (m) Changing the OpenHours name, primary domain, branding, or production
      deployment platform in a manner visible to users.
  (n) Approving press, marketing, fundraising, regulatory, or pitch-deck
      claims that purport to describe OpenHours's policies, security
      posture, compliance status, financial condition, or user metrics.
  (o) Engaging an external auditor, certifying body, penetration-testing
      firm, or law firm in the name of OpenHours.
  (p) Disclosing any portion of the Software's source code, internal
      analytics, security details, or unreleased product plans outside the
      Maintainer group, except as expressly permitted by this license, the
      Privacy Policy, or applicable law.

A Major Decision is "documented" when the Maintainers' agreement is
recorded in a written record that (i) names the decision, (ii) names each
consenting Maintainer, (iii) is dated, and (iv) is preserved in a place the
Maintainers reasonably agree to (including, by default, this repository's
commit history, a shared decision log, or signed email).

2.4 No Unilateral Action on Major Decisions.
A purported act, agreement, license, transfer, statement, or representation
by fewer than all three Maintainers that constitutes or would effect a
Major Decision is void, not binding on OpenHours, and not binding on the
non-consenting Maintainers. A third party that knew or reasonably should
have known of the unanimity requirement may not rely on a Major Decision
purportedly made without unanimous Maintainer consent.

2.5 Deadlock Resolution.
If the Maintainers cannot agree on a proposed Major Decision after good-
faith discussion for at least thirty (30) days, the proposed Major
Decision is rejected and the status quo prevails. Any Maintainer may, at
any time, request non-binding mediation by a mutually selected neutral
third party; the Maintainers will share the mediator's reasonable cost
equally. Repeated rejection of a proposed Major Decision is not, by itself,
grounds for removal under Section 3.4.

2.6 Confidentiality of Internal Decisions.
The Maintainers will treat each other's internal discussions about Major
Decisions, security incidents, financial information, prospective hires or
contractors, unreleased product plans, and user-specific data as
confidential. No Maintainer will disclose such information outside the
Maintainer group without unanimous consent, except (a) as required by law
or valid legal process, (b) to professional advisors (such as legal counsel
or accountants) who are bound by confidentiality, or (c) as necessary to
operate the Service consistent with the Privacy Policy.

2.7 Books, Records, and Access.
Each Maintainer is entitled to reasonable access to the operational records
of OpenHours, including source code, deployment configuration, hosting
dashboards, sub-processor accounts, and financial records (if any). No
Maintainer may unilaterally lock another Maintainer out of any system that
is necessary to participate in governance under this Section 2.

2.8 Voluntary Contributions of Time and Effort.
No Maintainer is required to contribute any minimum amount of time, labor,
or attention to OpenHours. Contributions of time and effort are voluntary.
Without limiting the generality of the foregoing, Colin Mcdonald may be
inactive during academic or employment commitments without penalty, removal,
forfeiture, or loss of ownership, and the same principle applies to each
other Maintainer.

2.9 No Financial Obligation.
No Maintainer is obligated to contribute funds, incur expenses, advance
capital, guarantee any obligation of OpenHours, or assume any financial
liability on behalf of OpenHours or the other Maintainers, unless that
Maintainer separately agrees in writing to do so. A Maintainer's voluntary
payment of an expense on one occasion does not create an obligation to do
so on any future occasion.

2.10 No Individual Operational Responsibility.
No Maintainer is individually responsible for legal compliance, regulatory
filings, user support, privacy operations, security operations, incident
response, accounting, tax, or any other operational obligation of
OpenHours, unless that Maintainer explicitly assumes the responsibility in
writing. Responsibilities not so assumed are joint responsibilities of the
active Maintainers, to be allocated by unanimous agreement.

2.11 No Unilateral Binding Acts.
No Maintainer may, acting alone, and without the unanimous, documented
written consent of the Maintainers:

  (a) enter into any contract, agreement, memorandum of understanding, or
      similar binding instrument on behalf of OpenHours;
  (b) incur expenses chargeable to OpenHours or to the other Maintainers;
  (c) onboard users to, or grant production access in, the production
      Service; or
  (d) collect, process, store, or transmit real user data on behalf of
      OpenHours.

A purported act in violation of this Section 2.11 is void as against
OpenHours and the non-consenting Maintainers, who incur no liability by
reason of it. This Section 2.11 supplements, and does not limit, the
unanimity requirements of Section 2.3.

================================================================================
3. Maintainer Changes
================================================================================

3.1 Voluntary Departure.
A Maintainer may withdraw from active day-to-day participation in OpenHours
at any time by giving written notice to the other Maintainers. Withdrawal
does not, by itself, transfer the withdrawing Maintainer's ownership share
or release them from obligations that survive under this license.

3.2 Effect of Departure.
A Maintainer who has withdrawn under Section 3.1:

  (a) ceases to be eligible to perform Routine Operations under
      Section 2.2;
  (b) remains required to consent to Major Decisions under Section 2.3
      until a Permitted Transfer of their share is completed under
      Section 3.3, except that consent may not be unreasonably withheld for
      Major Decisions necessary to preserve the Service, comply with law,
      or respond to a security incident;
  (c) remains bound by the confidentiality obligations in Section 2.6 with
      respect to information learned during their tenure for a period of
      five (5) years after withdrawal; and
  (d) loses access to production credentials, deployment dashboards, sub-
      processor accounts, and similar operational privileges, which the
      remaining Maintainers may revoke immediately on or after the
      effective date of withdrawal.

3.3 Permitted Transfer.
A Maintainer may transfer or assign their ownership share only with the
unanimous, documented written consent of the other Maintainers. As a
condition of the transfer, the incoming holder must sign a written
agreement (i) adopting this license without modification, (ii) accepting
the obligations of a Maintainer, and (iii) consenting to be bound by the
governance provisions of Section 2 and the Confidentiality obligations of
Section 2.6. An attempted transfer that does not meet these conditions is
void.

3.4 Removal For Cause.
A Maintainer may be removed from this license, and their decision rights
under Section 2 suspended, only by the unanimous, documented consent of
the other Maintainers and only on the basis of one or more of the
following:

  (a) Material breach of this license that remains uncured after thirty
      (30) days' written notice describing the breach;
  (b) Fraud, willful misconduct, embezzlement, or felony conviction
      related to OpenHours or its users;
  (c) Sustained inability or unwillingness to participate in the project
      for at least one hundred eighty (180) consecutive days following
      written notice; or
  (d) A material breach of confidentiality under Section 2.6 that causes,
      or is reasonably likely to cause, harm to OpenHours or its users.

Removal does not, by itself, divest the removed Maintainer of their
ownership share. Disposition of that share is subject to Section 3.3 or to
a separate written settlement agreed unanimously among the original
Maintainers.

3.5 Death or Incapacity.
On the death or legal incapacity of a Maintainer, the remaining Maintainers
will negotiate in good faith with the deceased or incapacitated
Maintainer's estate, executor, or legal representative to address the
disposition of that Maintainer's share. Pending resolution, the remaining
Maintainers may continue Routine Operations and may take Major Decisions
that are reasonably necessary to preserve the Service, protect users, or
comply with law, but may not take Major Decisions that materially prejudice
the absent Maintainer's economic interest.

3.6 Limit on Maintainer Count.
Without unanimous, documented consent under Section 2.3(i), the number of
Maintainers will not exceed three at any time.

3.7 Inactive Status.
A Maintainer may elect inactive status at any time by giving written notice
to the other Maintainers. During inactive status:

  (a) the inactive Maintainer has no obligation to participate in Routine
      Operations under Section 2.2;
  (b) the inactive Maintainer has no obligation to respond to, deliberate
      on, or vote on any proposed Major Decision under Section 2.3; and
  (c) the inactive Maintainer's non-response does not block a proposed
      Major Decision, and Section 2.3 is, with respect to that Maintainer
      only and for the duration of inactive status, treated as satisfied if
      the proposed Major Decision is unanimously consented to by the
      remaining active Maintainers, provided that the inactive Maintainer
      received reasonable written notice (not less than fourteen (14) days)
      of the proposed Major Decision and did not affirmatively object.

Notwithstanding clause (c), the affirmative written consent of the inactive
Maintainer remains required for any Major Decision that (i) transfers,
encumbers, dilutes, or otherwise materially diminishes the inactive
Maintainer's ownership share or economic interest, (ii) removes the
inactive Maintainer under Section 3.4, or (iii) imposes financial,
indemnification, or personal-liability obligations on the inactive
Maintainer individually. An inactive Maintainer retains their full
ownership share and confidentiality obligations and may return to active
status at any time by written notice to the other Maintainers.

3.8 Resignation with Relinquishment of Decision Rights.
In addition to withdrawal under Section 3.1 and inactive status under
Section 3.7, a Maintainer may, by written notice to the other Maintainers,
resign and permanently relinquish all decision-making rights under
Section 2 (including the right to consent to or block Major Decisions)
while either (a) retaining their ownership share or (b) transferring it
pursuant to a Permitted Transfer under Section 3.3. A Maintainer who has
resigned under this Section 3.8:

  (a) is released from any ongoing obligation to participate in Routine
      Operations or governance, including any obligation to consent to
      Major Decisions, except that the affirmative written consent of the
      resigning Maintainer remains required for any Major Decision that
      transfers, encumbers, dilutes, or otherwise materially diminishes
      their ownership share or economic interest for so long as they
      retain that share;
  (b) remains bound by the confidentiality obligations of Section 2.6 on
      the terms set out in Section 3.2(c); and
  (c) is treated, for purposes of any unanimity requirement in this
      license, as not counted in the quorum, such that unanimous consent
      of the remaining Maintainers satisfies that requirement, subject to
      clause (a) above.

Resignation under this Section 3.8 is irrevocable as to the relinquished
decision rights; a former Maintainer who wishes to resume governance may
do so only by being readmitted as a Maintainer under Section 3.3 with the
unanimous, documented consent of the then-current Maintainers.

